### How does the Factory pattern work?

Factory has one product interface and one factory method that returns one product.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public interface PaymentProcessor {
    void process(BigDecimal amount);
}

public class UpiProcessor implements PaymentProcessor {
    public void process(BigDecimal amount) {
        System.out.println("UPI payment: " + amount);
    }
}

public class CardProcessor implements PaymentProcessor {
    public void process(BigDecimal amount) {
        System.out.println("Card payment: " + amount);
    }
}

public class ProcessorFactory {
    public static PaymentProcessor create(String type) {
        return switch (type) {
            case "UPI" -> new UpiProcessor();
            case "CARD" -> new CardProcessor();
            default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown: " + type);
        };
    }
}
```

The decision is "which one concrete class." Adding a new payment type is one class and one branch.

### How does the Abstract Factory pattern work?

Abstract Factory has multiple product interfaces and a factory interface with one method per product. Each concrete factory produces a complete, consistent family.

```java
package com.bank.report;

// Product interfaces
public interface ReportHeader {
    String render();
}

public interface ReportFormatter {
    String format(String content);
}

public interface ReportFooter {
    String render();
}

// Abstract Factory interface
public interface DocumentFactory {
    ReportHeader createHeader();
    ReportFormatter createFormatter();
    ReportFooter createFooter();
}

// Regulatory family
public class RegulatoryHeader implements ReportHeader {
    public String render() { return "[REGULATORY FILING - RBI]"; }
}
public class RegulatoryFormatter implements ReportFormatter {
    public String format(String c) { return "FORMAL: " + c; }
}
public class RegulatoryFooter implements ReportFooter {
    public String render() { return "Certified by National Bank of India"; }
}
public class RegulatoryDocFactory implements DocumentFactory {
    public ReportHeader createHeader() { return new RegulatoryHeader(); }
    public ReportFormatter createFormatter() { return new RegulatoryFormatter(); }
    public ReportFooter createFooter() { return new RegulatoryFooter(); }
}

// Customer family
public class CustomerHeader implements ReportHeader {
    public String render() { return "Dear Valued Customer,"; }
}
public class CustomerFormatter implements ReportFormatter {
    public String format(String c) { return "Summary: " + c; }
}
public class CustomerFooter implements ReportFooter {
    public String render() { return "Thank you for banking with us!"; }
}
public class CustomerDocFactory implements DocumentFactory {
    public ReportHeader createHeader() { return new CustomerHeader(); }
    public ReportFormatter createFormatter() { return new CustomerFormatter(); }
    public ReportFooter createFooter() { return new CustomerFooter(); }
}
```

### What is the structural difference?

| Aspect | Factory | Abstract Factory |
|---|---|---|
| Products | One product hierarchy | Multiple related product hierarchies |
| Question | Which one type? | Which matching family? |
| Output | One object | A set of consistent objects |
| Client depends on | Interface + factory | Abstract factory + abstract products |
| Swap | One product changes | Entire family changes at once |

### How does the client use Abstract Factory?

The client depends only on the abstract factory and product interfaces. Switching the entire family is a one-line change.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class ReportGenerator {
    private final DocumentFactory factory;

    public ReportGenerator(DocumentFactory factory) {
        this.factory = factory;
    }

    public void generate(String content) {
        System.out.println(factory.createHeader().render());
        System.out.println(factory.createFormatter().format(content));
        System.out.println(factory.createFooter().render());
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class AbstractFactoryDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Regulatory report
        ReportGenerator regGen = new ReportGenerator(new RegulatoryDocFactory());
        regGen.generate("Q4 compliance data");

        System.out.println();

        // Customer report – swap factory, everything changes
        ReportGenerator custGen = new ReportGenerator(new CustomerDocFactory());
        custGen.generate("Your account summary");
    }
}
```

```text
[REGULATORY FILING - RBI]
FORMAL: Q4 compliance data
Certified by National Bank of India

Dear Valued Customer,
Summary: Your account summary
Thank you for banking with us!
```

### When do you use Factory vs Abstract Factory?

- Use **Factory** when you have one product type whose concrete class varies: picking a settlement handler by trade type.
- Use **Abstract Factory** when several products must vary together as a consistent set: a document with matching header, formatter, and footer.

### What are common mistakes?

- Using Abstract Factory when products do not need to match: a set of independent factory methods is simpler.
- Mixing products from different families: a regulatory header with a customer footer produces an invalid document.

### How do both patterns simplify in modern Java?

In modern Java, a Factory Method often collapses to a `Map<String, Supplier<T>>` lookup, and an Abstract Factory collapses to a record or interface returning lambdas. Sealed interfaces with pattern matching make the type hierarchy explicit and the factory switch expression exhaustive.

```java
package com.bank.pattern;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.function.Supplier;

public class ModernFactoryDemo {
    // Modern Factory: Map<String, Supplier>
    sealed interface FeeCalculator permits ProcessingFee, LateFee {
        BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal principal);
    }
    record ProcessingFee() implements FeeCalculator {
        public BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal p) { return p.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.01")); }
    }
    record LateFee() implements FeeCalculator {
        public BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal p) { return p.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.02")); }
    }

    static final Map<String, Supplier<FeeCalculator>> FACTORY = Map.of(
        "PROCESSING", ProcessingFee::new,
        "LATE", LateFee::new
    );

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        FeeCalculator calc = FACTORY.get("PROCESSING").get();
        System.out.println("Fee: " + calc.calculate(new BigDecimal("100000")));
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Fee: 1000.00
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Abstract Factory is more machinery than Factory. Only use it when family consistency genuinely matters.
- In Spring, you can implement Abstract Factory by injecting different configuration profiles.
- Abstract Factory is sometimes called "factory of factories" because each method inside it is its own factory method.

```java
// Senior tip: if products don't need to match,
// just use separate Factory methods
// HeaderFactory.create(type)
// FormatterFactory.create(type)
// Simpler and sufficient when consistency doesn't matter
```
